  "Number": 13,
  "Question": "The function \\(x^2+px+q\\) with \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) greater than zero has its minimum value when:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(x=-p\\)",
    "B": "\\(x=\\dfrac p2\\)",
    "C": "\\(x=-2p\\)",
    "D": "\\(x=\\dfrac{p^2}{4q}\\)",
    "E": "\\(x=-\\dfrac p2\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Completing the square reveals which part of the expression can vary and how small it can become. For real \\(x\\),\n\\[\nx^2+px+q=\\left(x+\\frac p2\\right)^2+q-\\frac{p^2}{4}.\n\\]\nThe square is nonnegative, so the function is at least \\(q-\\frac{p^2}{4}\\). It attains that value exactly when \\(x+\\frac p2=0\\). Therefore the minimum occurs at \\(x=-\\frac p2\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
